1
00:00:00,410 --> 00:00:03,150
He moved someone else’s
finger with his mind.

2
00:00:03,450 --> 00:00:06,190
Watch the hand on the left.
That’s the guy playing.

3
00:00:06,410 --> 00:00:08,880
The finger on the right
just fired his cannon.

4
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:12,280
It belongs to a man across campus
who can’t even see the game.

5
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:16,780
In 2013, two researchers
at the University of Washington

6
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:19,640
split one video game
between two buildings.

7
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:21,590
Rajesh Rao got the screen.

8
00:00:22,070 --> 00:00:24,060
Andrea Stocco got the space bar.

9
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:28,550
Together, they had the equipment you
normally get by sitting down at a desk.

10
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:31,530
Rao’s job was to defend a little city.

11
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:34,250
A supply plane flies over? Let it through.

12
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:37,970
A pirate ship launches
a rocket? Shoot it down.

13
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:41,280
Easy enough, except the only
person who could see

14
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:44,250
the difference had no way
to press fire himself.

15
00:00:45,090 --> 00:00:47,140
Watch what happens on a rocket.

16
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:49,640
Rao imagines moving his right hand.

17
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:51,460
His real hand stays put.

18
00:00:51,710 --> 00:00:55,290
But under that cap, the rhythm
of his brain activity changes.

19
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:57,690
A computer is watching for that change.

20
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,420
On his screen, a little cursor climbs.

21
00:01:00,970 --> 00:01:04,610
When it hits the target, the computer
sends one command over the internet.

22
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:06,130
Fire.

23
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:09,770
Across campus, a magnetic
coil sits over the part

24
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:12,390
of Stocco’s brain that
helps move his right hand.

25
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:14,420
The command triggers a pulse.

26
00:01:14,630 --> 00:01:18,770
His hand jerks up, comes down on
the space bar, and the cannon shoots.

27
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:21,770
The rocket disappears on Rao’s screen.

28
00:01:22,070 --> 00:01:25,380
Stocco described the feeling
as something like a nervous tic.

29
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:29,270
Imagine being the controller
and missing the entire game.

30
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:33,770
There’s a detail I love
in their pilot report.

31
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:37,170
Their first session ended early
because of network problems.

32
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:39,520
Two human brains, ready to go.

33
00:01:40,130 --> 00:01:41,140
Bad connection.

34
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:45,930
A year later, the team tried it
with three pairs of volunteers.

35
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:49,200
This time the receiver’s
hand rested over a touchpad.

36
00:01:49,830 --> 00:01:53,310
In some rounds, the researchers
quietly flipped the coil over.

37
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:55,660
Same game. Same pulse strength.

38
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:59,040
Even the participants couldn’t
tell which way the coil was facing.

39
00:01:59,990 --> 00:02:03,970
With the coil in position, one pair
hit about five out of six rockets.

40
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:05,940
Another hit only one in four.

41
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:07,790
With the coil flipped?

42
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:13,310
Across all three pairs,
nobody pressed the touchpad once.

43
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:14,940
Every rocket got through.

44
00:02:16,370 --> 00:02:20,750
Then the researchers gave the person
on the receiving end a much better job.

45
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:23,530
In a 2019 study, three people played

46
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:26,580
a falling-block game
from three separate rooms.

47
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:29,130
Two players could see
the gap at the bottom.

48
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:33,080
The third could see the block and had
to decide whether to rotate it.

49
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:36,350
From that seat, either
answer looked reasonable.

50
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:38,860
Each teammate sent a vote.

51
00:02:39,550 --> 00:02:42,860
This time the coil was at the back
of the receiver’s head,

52
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:45,410
over the part of the brain
involved in vision.

53
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:48,410
A little flash in their
vision meant “rotate.”

54
00:02:48,630 --> 00:02:50,660
No flash meant “leave it.”

55
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:54,840
They got one vote from each
teammate, chose what to do,

56
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:58,520
then got a fresh round of votes
and a chance to change their answer.

57
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:02,110
Here’s where I’d start
having trust issues.

58
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:07,220
On ten of the sixteen trials, the computer
flipped one teammate’s advice.

59
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:10,760
Over time, receivers learned
whose signal to trust.

60
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:15,380
Across five teams, they cleared the line
about thirteen times out of sixteen.

61
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:17,520
Go back to that finger.

62
00:03:18,330 --> 00:03:22,200
In the first game, the pulse helped
decide when somebody else’s hand moved.

63
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:25,710
Six years later, a pulse
could give you advice.

64
00:03:26,490 --> 00:03:28,720
And you could decide
the other guy was wrong.
